From fb2458886f74f10a9799fb9d40bb026d95f8d36b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DisplayXR <dev@displayxr.invalid>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 16:24:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#171): hold the whole-panel FLAT until the new
 page's first weave submit

Navigating back in the gallery or the storefront briefly shows a 2D element
"woven". Reproduced with --v=1 on the gallery (SPA back, full back, from the
top and from a scrolled wall) and read against the service log. It is neither
the page nor the SDK: it is the panel's sticky 3D wish carrying the PREVIOUS
page's tile rect across the navigation.

THE MECHANISM, in the service's own words. Every navigation drops inline-3D
demand (the old session ends), which since browser#159 latches a whole-panel
FLAT, then raises it again for the new page, which clears the latch:

  16:11:06.557  [weave_wish] sticky screen flat regions latched: 1 rect(s)
  16:11:06.557  ZONES SVC: wish mask ... 1 3D rect(s) - 1 flat rect(s)
  16:11:07.105  [app_mode] ... hw_req=1 -> 3D=1
  16:11:07.127  [weave_wish] sticky screen flat regions latched: 0 rect(s)
  16:11:07.127  ZONES SVC: wish mask ... 1 3D rect(s) - 0 flat rect(s)
  16:11:07.568  inline-3D layers: canvas_res=1     <- new page's tile, only now

The runtime services the clear by republishing "from the LAST submitted 3D
rects -- they are still the active geometry; only the flat set moved"
(comp_d3d11_service.cpp ~21209). Across a navigation that is false: the last
submitted rects are the previous page's. For ~0.4 s the lens is ON over a rect
the new page has not woven, and whatever 2D sits there -- a card, a header, a
product photo -- is seen through it. "Occasional" because it depends on what
the new page has under the stale rect and on how long its first tile takes to
resolve.

The browser cannot retract the set: a zero-rect batch submit is refused at the
OpenXR layer (oxr_weave.c, rectCount < 1); the runtime's "zero-rect submit is
a withdrawal" is the legacy single-rect path. What the browser CAN choose is
WHEN the flat is released.

THE FIX, entirely in the GPU-side weave client, no mojo or runtime change.
SetScreenFlatRegionsWin becomes a policy wrapper over the raw applier:

  * a set that ADDS the whole-panel rect is applied immediately -- a flatten
    is always safe -- and supersedes any held release;
  * a set that REMOVES it while the runtime holds it is HELD, and released
    right after the next successful non-empty batch submit (posted from the
    present thread back to the receiving sequence), at which point "the last
    submitted 3D rects" are the new page's by construction;
  * a held release is generation-guarded: any newer set supersedes it, so a
    demand that drops again before the page weaves is never un-flattened by a
    stale release arriving late;
  * the three re-negotiation re-send sites use the raw applier, so a
    reconnect cannot cancel a held release and strand the panel flat.

A page that demands 3D but never submits stays FLAT -- the correct rendering
of a page that is not weaving.

WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE. A popup opening or closing while a page weaves (demand
steady, no whole-panel rect latched) applies immediately, exactly as before.
The tracker (browser process) and the runtime are untouched.

Two VLOG(1) lines name the hold and the release so the next log reads the
transition instead of inferring it.
